COMPLETE REVISION SHEET

1. CLASSIFICATION OF SOLIDS AND ENERGY BAND THEORY
- Group 14 Elements: Carbon (C), Silicon (Si), Germanium (Ge) contain 4 valence e⁻.
- Carbon Exception: Small atomic size creates extremely strong covalent bonds that cannot be broken at room temp without destroying the lattice; behaves as an insulator.
- Silicon & Germanium: Larger atomic radii lead to weaker covalent bonds easily broken by thermal or EM energy to liberate conduction e⁻.
- Conduction-Based Classification: Conductors (electron sea, high σ, low ρ: Au, Ag, Cu); Insulators (no free e⁻, extremely low σ); Semiconductors (insulator at 0 K, thermal bond breaking enables conduction at room temp).
- Energy Bands: Isolated atoms = sharp discrete levels E = -13.6 Z² / n² eV. Solid lattice = outermost valence shell levels spread into continuous energy bands due to interatomic forces; inner shell energies remain intact.
- Valence Band (VB): lower energy band containing bound valence e⁻ in bonds. Conduction Band (CB): higher energy band representing free e⁻ liberated from bonds.
- Band Gap ΔE_g = E_C - E_V; forbidden energy range between CB minimum (E_C) and VB maximum (E_V).
- Band Theory Classification:
  - Conductors: VB and CB overlap (ΔE_g = 0); valence e⁻ directly conduct.
  - Insulators: ΔE_g ≥ 3 eV; 📌 thermal energy to cross ΔE_g exceeds lattice binding energy → lattice breakdown/melting before conduction.
  - Semiconductors: ΔE_g ≈ 1 eV; Si ΔE_g = 1.1 eV; Ge ΔE_g = 0.7 eV.
- Threshold Wavelength: ⭐ λ_max = 1240 / ΔE_g(in eV) nm; maximum photon wavelength to promote e⁻ across ΔE_g.

2. HOLES AND CARRIER TRANSPORT
- Hole Creation: Thermal breaking of bond promotes e⁻ to CB, leaving vacant site in VB = Hole.
- Hole Transport: Under field E, bound e⁻ in adjacent bonds hop into vacant hole site sequentially → hole moves in direction of E.
- Hole Properties:
  1. Virtual charge +e; no physical positive particle.
  2. Transport in VB via bound e⁻ hopping; free e⁻ transport in CB independently.
  3. Effective Mass m_h* > m_e* (m* = ℏ² / (d²E/dk²)) due to atomic potential barriers hindering bound e⁻ hopping.
  4. Mobility μ_h < μ_e.
- Total Current: ⭐ I = I_e + I_h; sum of free e⁻ current in CB (I_e) and hole current in VB (I_h).

3. INTRINSIC AND EXTRINSIC SEMICONDUCTORS
- Intrinsic: Pure Si/Ge without impurities. Perfect insulator at 0 K.
  ⭐ Carrier Equality: n_e = n_h = n_i.
  ⭐ Mass Action Law: n_i² = n_e · n_h; 📌 condition: thermal equilibrium.
  - Limitations: Current small, uncontrolled, T-dependent; carrier types cannot be varied independently.
- Doping & Extrinsic: Deliberate addition of dopant impurities to boost conductivity.
  📌 Doping Conditions: Size matching host atom; low concentration (ppm/ppb: 1 in 10⁶ to 10⁹); substitutional occupancy.
- P-Type:
  - Group 13 trivalent dopants (B, Al, Ga, In); forms 3 bonds, 1 vacant site = hole.
  - Acceptor Ion: B accepts e⁻ → becomes immobile negative ion B⁻.
  - Carriers: Majority = Holes (n_h ≈ N_A); Minority = Free e⁻ (n_e = n_i² / N_A).
  - Energy Level: Acceptor level E_A slightly above VB top E_V.
  ⚠️ Bulk Neutrality: Bulk P-type is strictly ELECTRICALLY NEUTRAL.
- N-Type:
  - Group 15 pentavalent dopants (P, As, Sb); forms 4 bonds, 5th e⁻ weakly bound, easily liberated to CB.
  - Donor Ion: P donates e⁻ → becomes immobile positive ion P⁺.
  - Carriers: Majority = Free e⁻ (n_e ≈ N_D); Minority = Holes (n_h = n_i² / N_D).
  - Energy Level: Donor level E_D slightly below CB bottom E_C.
  ⚠️ Bulk Neutrality: Bulk N-type is strictly ELECTRICALLY NEUTRAL.

4. ELECTRICAL CONDUCTIVITY
- Microscopic Current Density: ⭐ J = e · (n_e · v_de + n_h · v_dh) = e · E · (n_e · μ_e + n_h · μ_h); e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.
- Conductivity: ⭐ σ = 1/ρ = e · (n_e · μ_e + n_h · μ_h); units: σ in S/m or Ω⁻¹·m⁻¹, ρ in Ω·m, μ in m²/(V·s).
  - Intrinsic: σ = e · n_i · (μ_e + μ_h).
  - P-Type: σ ≈ e · N_A · μ_h.
  - N-Type: σ ≈ e · N_D · μ_e.
- Temperature Coefficient α: Conductors α > 0 (heating increases lattice scattering); Semiconductors α < 0 (heating exponentially increases n_e, n_h via bond breaking, overriding scattering).

5. PN JUNCTION AND DEPLETION LAYER
- Assembly: Joining P & N blocks (Al foil interface heated to ~500 °C for continuous lattice).
- Formation Steps:
  1. Diffusion: Steep concentration gradient drives holes P → N and e⁻ N → P; creates Diffusion Current I_diff from P to N.
  2. Uncovering Ions: Recombination near interface exposes immobile ions (B⁻ on P-side, P⁺ on N-side).
  3. Depletion Layer: Central region depleted of mobile carriers, containing only fixed space charge ions.
  4. Built-in Field & Barrier Potential: Immobile ions create internal field E_int from N to P; ⭐ V_B = E_int · l; Si V_B ≈ 0.7 V, Ge V_B ≈ 0.3 V at room temp.
  5. Drift: E_int sweeps minority carriers across junction; creates Drift Current I_drift from N to P.
  6. Dynamic Equilibrium: Depletion width grows until |I_diff| = |I_drift|; net current = 0 across unbiased junction.
- Asymmetric Doping Penetration:
  - Charge conservation: q · N_A · x_P = q · N_D · x_N.
  - Penetration ratio: ⭐ x_P / x_N = N_D / N_A; depletion region extends deeper into lighter-doped side (e.g., P⁺N → depletion mostly in N-side).

6. BIASING MECHANISMS AND V-I CHARACTERISTICS
- Biasing Check Rule: ⭐ ΔV_bias = V_P - V_N; ΔV_bias > 0 → Forward Bias; ΔV_bias < 0 → Reverse Bias; ΔV_bias = 0 → Unbiased.
- Forward Bias: P to (+), N to (-); E_ext opposes E_int; depletion width l decreases, barrier potential decreases to (V_B - V_ext); conduction when V_ext > V_B; majority carrier current in mA; ideal equivalent = closed switch / short wire.
- Reverse Bias: P to (-), N to (+); E_ext aids E_int; depletion width l increases, barrier potential increases to (V_B + V_ext); majority flow blocked; thermally generated minority carriers swept across forming Reverse Saturation Current I_S in nA.
  📌 Voltage Independence: I_S is independent of V_ext (limited by thermal generation rate); I_S doubles approximately every 10 °C rise in T; ideal equivalent = open switch / break (I = 0, R = ∞).
- V-I Characteristics & Dynamic Resistance:
  - Knee voltage V_k: Si V_k ≈ 0.7 V, Ge V_k ≈ 0.3 V; exponential current rise above V_k.
  - Reverse breakdown voltage V_Z: High field ruptures bonds → current spikes.
  - Dynamic Resistance: ⭐ R_dyn = ΔV / ΔI = 1 / slope; non-ohmic element; low in FB above V_k (~10-100 Ω); extremely high in RB (~10⁶ Ω).

7. DIODE CIRCUIT ANALYSIS STRATEGIES
- Ideal Diode Replacement: FB → short-circuit wire; RB → open-circuit break.
- Real Diode Replacement (FB): Knee voltage V_k → replace diode with opposing battery V_k (positive facing P-side); Forward resistance R_f → add series resistor R_f. V_net = V_supply - V_k; I = (V_supply - V_k) / (R_total + R_f).
💡 Shortcut: Erase reverse-biased diode branches completely from schematic during analysis.

8. APPLICATIONS OF PN JUNCTION DIODE
- Rectifiers: Converts AC to unidirectional pulsating DC.
  - Half-Wave Rectifier: 1 diode, conducts during positive half-cycle; efficiency η = 40.6%; output ripple frequency f_out = f_in; T_out = T_in.
  - Full-Wave Center-Tapped Rectifier: 2 diodes (D₁, D₂), center-tapped secondary; efficiency η = 81.2%; output ripple frequency f_out = 2 · f_in; T_out = T_in / 2.
- Capacitor Filter: Parallel C across R_L; X_C = 1 / (2πfC); DC (f = 0) → X_C = ∞ (blocks DC, forces through R_L); AC (f > 0) → X_C small (bypasses AC to ground).
- Optoelectronic Diodes:
  - Photo Diode: Strictly REVERSE BIAS; detects optical signals/intensity; photons (h·ν ≥ ΔE_g) break bonds in depletion layer → e⁻-h⁺ pairs swept by E_int → reverse current increases; photo-current ∝ incident light intensity; 📌 sensitivity reason: fractional change ΔI / I_reverse in RB is massive & easily detectable compared to FB; threshold λ_max = 1240 / ΔE_g(eV) nm.
  - LED: Strictly FORWARD BIAS; converts electrical energy to light; FB injects majority carriers → radiative recombination in depletion layer → emits photons; band gap requirement: 1.8 eV ≤ ΔE_g ≤ 3.1 eV (4000 Å to 8000 Å, e.g., GaAsP).
  - Solar Cell: UNBIASED (V_ext = 0); converts solar energy to electricity; thin top P-layer allows sunlight to reach depletion layer; photons (h·ν ≥ ΔE_g) create e⁻-h⁺ pairs → separated by E_int (e⁻ to N, h⁺ to P) → generates open-circuit photovoltage V_oc.
- Zener Diode Voltage Regulator: Heavily doped, thin depletion layer (~10⁻⁶ m), high E_int (~10⁶ V/m); operates safely in reverse breakdown.
  - Circuit: REVERSE BIAS in parallel with R_L across unregulated V_in with series resistor R_s.
  📌 Regulation Condition: V_in > V_Z.
  - Equations: V_L = V_Z; I_L = V_Z / R_L; I_total = (V_in - V_Z) / R_s; ⭐ I_Z = I_total - I_L; P_Z = V_Z · I_Z.

9. LOGIC GATES AND DIGITAL ELECTRONICS
- Digital Signal Levels: Low (0 V) = Logic 0; High (+5 V) = Logic 1; n binary inputs → combinations = 2ⁿ.
- Basic Gates:
  - OR Gate: Y = A + B; Y = 1 if ANY input is 1; Y = 0 only if ALL 0.
  - AND Gate: Y = A · B; Y = 1 ONLY if ALL inputs 1; Y = 0 if ANY 0.
  - NOT Gate: Y = A_bar; single-input inverter.
- Universal Gates:
  - NAND Gate: Y = (A · B)_bar; Y = 0 ONLY if ALL inputs 1; Y = 1 otherwise.
  - NOR Gate: Y = (A + B)_bar; Y = 1 ONLY if ALL inputs 0; Y = 0 otherwise.
  💡 Single-Input Conversion: Tying inputs together converts NAND or NOR to NOT gate (Y = A_bar).
- Special Gates:
  - XOR Gate: Y = A_bar · B + A · B_bar = A ⊕ B; Y = 1 when inputs DIFFERENT; Y = 0 when IDENTICAL.
  - XNOR Gate: Y = A · B + A_bar · B_bar = A ⊙ B; Y = 1 when inputs IDENTICAL; Y = 0 when DIFFERENT.
- Boolean Algebra & De Morgan's Laws:
  - Additive: A + 0 = A; A + 1 = 1; A + A = A.
  - Multiplicative: A · 0 = 0; A · 1 = A; A · A = A.
  - Complement: A + A_bar = 1; A · A_bar = 0; A_bar_bar = A.
  ⭐ De Morgan's First Law: (A + B)_bar = A_bar · B_bar.
  ⭐ De Morgan's Second Law: (A · B)_bar = A_bar + B_bar.

